@page "/toolings/code-analyzers"
@inherits AppComponentBase

<PageOutlet Url="toolings/code-analyzers"
            Title="bit Code Analyzers - Toolings"
            Description="bit Code Analyzers turn the bit platform team's rules for quality code into build errors and warnings, shown as you type, for every developer and every AI assistant." />

<div class="page-container">
    <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H3" Element="h1" Gutter>bit Code Analyzers</BitText>
    <br />
    <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>Mistakes caught while you type, not after you ship</BitText>
    <br />
    <BitText Typography="BitTypography.Subtitle1" Gutter>
        We care about code quality, and a rule that lives only in a review checklist gets missed sooner or later.
        bit Code Analyzers put our rules into the compiler instead: the build fails or warns, right in the editor,
        whether the code was written by a new teammate, a senior developer or an AI assistant.
    </BitText>
    <CodeBox>dotnet add package Bit.CodeAnalyzers</CodeBox>
    Every bit Boilerplate project has it already.
    <br />
    <br />

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>Why rules belong in the compiler</BitText>
            <ul>
                <li><b>The same rules for everyone.</b> People and AI assistants get the same feedback, in the editor and in CI.</li>
                <li><b>Right away.</b> A mistake shows while the code is still on the screen, not in a review days later.</li>
                <li><b>Cheaper than a bug.</b> An app crash or a server that stops scaling is found in seconds, before it reaches production.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>No async void</BitText>
            <code>AsyncVoidMethodAnalyzer1</code> and <code>AsyncVoidMethodAnalyzer2</code>, errors.
            <br />
            <br />
            An exception thrown in an <code>async void</code> method can't be caught by its caller, so it takes the whole app down.
            Return a <code>Task</code> instead:
            <CodeBox HideCopyButton>async Task SaveAsync()
{
    await productController.Update(product, cancellationToken);
}</CodeBox>
            When it has to be <code>async void</code>, like an event handler, the whole body goes in a <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>,
            and a statement left outside the <code>try</code> is reported too:
            <CodeBox HideCopyButton>async void OnTimerElapsed(object? sender, ElapsedEventArgs e)
{
    try
    {
        await RefreshAsync();
    }
    catch (Exception exp)
    {
        exceptionHandler.Handle(exp);
    }
}</CodeBox>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>Async database calls</BitText>
            <code>NonAsyncEFCoreMethodsUsageAnalyzer</code>, warning.
            <br />
            <br />
            A synchronous Entity Framework Core call, like <code>ToList</code>, <code>First</code>, <code>Count</code> or <code>SaveChanges</code>,
            holds a thread while the database answers. Under load a server runs out of threads that way and stops scaling.
            The rule asks for the async twin of each one:
            <CodeBox HideCopyButton>var products = await dbContext.Products
    .Where(p => p.Price > 50)
    .ToListAsync(cancellationToken);</CodeBox>
            It looks at queries, <code>DbContext</code>, its <code>Database</code>, change tracking entries and context factories.
            A call inside an expression that EF Core turns into SQL, such as a <code>Count()</code> in a <code>Select</code>, isn't reported.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>Classes say who can use them</BitText>
            <code>ClassWithoutModifierAnalyzer</code>, error, with a quick fix.
            <br />
            <br />
            A class or record with no modifier at all is reported, so who can use it is always written down rather than left to the language's default.
            The editor's quick fix adds <code>public</code>.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>No internal accessors</BitText>
            <code>PropertyAccessorsInternalModifierAnalyzer</code>, error.
            <br />
            <br />
            A property accessor can't be <code>internal</code>, as in <code>public string Name { get; internal set; }</code>.
            JSON deserialization, for one, skips such a setter without a word and leaves the property empty.
            Make the whole property <code>internal</code>, or the accessor <code>private</code> or <code>protected</code>.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>Changing a rule's severity</BitText>
            Each rule's severity can be raised or lowered in <code>.editorconfig</code>, like any compiler rule:
            <CodeBox>[*.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.NonAsyncEFCoreMethodsUsageAnalyzer.severity = error</CodeBox>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

<NavigationButtons Prev="BlazorES2019" PrevUrl="@Urls.BlazorES2019" Next="Source Generators" NextUrl="@Urls.SourceGenerators" />
